BT /F2 14 Tf 0.02 0.45 0.50 rg 1 0 0 1 50 711.0 Tm (HISTORICAL CHRONOLOGY) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 676.0 Tm (The first Amiga machines shipped in 1985 with the Original File System \(OFS\). When larger) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 659.0 Tm (hard disks and applications required better sequential throughput, Amiga Fast File System) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 642.0 Tm (was introduced in the late 1980s and became associated with AmigaOS 1.3-era hard disk use.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 625.0 Tm (It was a storage format change, not simply a faster driver reading exactly the same OFS) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 608.0 Tm (file data blocks.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 578.0 Tm (Historical AmigaDOS types identify Fast File System variants: DOS1 for original FFS, DOS3) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 561.0 Tm (for international case handling and DOS5 for directory-cache support. Later long-name) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 544.0 Tm (extensions added further modes. The AmigaOS and Linux AFFS documents distinguish these) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 527.0 Tm (on-media variations and support limitations; a reader must detect the actual DOS type) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 510.0 Tm (before interpreting data.) Tj ET
