BT /F2 14 Tf 0.02 0.45 0.50 rg 1 0 0 1 50 711.0 Tm (ORIGINAL ON-DISK ARCHITECTURE) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 676.0 Tm (FFS preserves the Amiga model of boot blocks, root block, allocation bitmap, hashed) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 659.0 Tm (directory structures, file headers and extension blocks. The decisive difference from OFS) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 642.0 Tm (is that FFS ordinary file data blocks contain raw file payload rather than the 24-byte OFS) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 625.0 Tm (data header. A data block can therefore carry more useful bytes and be accessed more) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 608.0 Tm (efficiently.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 578.0 Tm (File header blocks and extension blocks contain the table of data-block pointers. Because) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 561.0 Tm (FFS does not retain OFS data-block headers containing reverse information, damaged file) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 544.0 Tm (headers may make recovery of payload ownership more difficult. The root and metadata) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 527.0 Tm (blocks still have structured fields and checksums, but ordinary FFS file payload blocks) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.07 0.16 0.23 rg 1 0 0 1 50 510.0 Tm (are not equivalent to checksummed OFS records.) Tj ET
